Second month of pregnancy\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa300\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf2 A pregnancy test will usually alert you to your pregnancy this month when your period doesn\'92t come. You may also feel very tired. This fatigue is caused by the hormones flooding your body as it prepares to help your baby grow. You may also notice that you have an increased need to urinate and may even experience heartburn or morning sickness (nausea and vomiting).\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 Third month of pregnancy\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa300\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf2 While the uterus has been growing, it won\'92t be until the end of this month that it reaches the top of your pelvis. You probably haven\'92t gained more than a pound or two and may have even lost a few pounds if you\'92ve been sick or having aversions to some foods. You may also experience a decrease in libido in this first trimester of pregnancy. Your skin may glow, and you may have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.acog.org/Patients/FAQs/Skin-Conditions-During-Pregnancy"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 dark blotches on your face or lots of acne}}.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 Fourth month of pregnancy\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa300\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf2 You have made it to the second trimester! This means that you\'92ll likely start feeling more like your old self. You may not need as many naps, your morning sickness seems to fade away, and you may feel a spring in your step. You may feel these positive changes because the worry of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.acog.org/Patients/FAQs/Early-Pregnancy-Loss"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 early miscarriage}} is over, or because your hormones are a bit less volatile as the placenta has taken over the production of some hormones to sustain the pregnancy. Your sex drive is usually back to prepregnancy levels.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 Fifth month of pregnancy\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa300\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf2 This is a big month. The expanded blood volume has your heart beating a bit faster. Your kidneys are working hard, which means you are still making frequent trips to the bathroom. This month is also when you\'92ll most likely {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.healthcentral.com/article/when-will-i-feel-the-baby-move-pregnancy"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 feel your baby move for the first time}}. Your uterus also reaches the point of your belly button, so you may have a few strangers take notice of your pregnancy.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 Sixth month of pregnancy\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa300\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf2 During this month of pregnancy, you likely feel great and are visibly pregnant. You may experience a bit more heartburn as your pregnant abdomen grows and makes less room in your stomach. You can also feel your baby more distinctly, which can be a lot of fun. Maternity clothes are most likely your primary wardrobe. You may also notice {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.acog.org/Patients/FAQs/Back-Pain-During-Pregnancy"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 mild backaches}} occasionally.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 Seventh month of pregnancy\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa300\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf2 The third trimester is here, and you\'92re in the home stretch. For some, this can be a shock. Pregnancy seems like it will last forever, and now you realize that it\'92s almost time for your baby to arrive. This may make you start thinking more about planning for labor and bringing your baby home. You may become less interested in sex as you worry about your growing belly and the fatigue starts to set in. You may also begin to have practice contractions known as Braxton-Hicks contractions.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 Eighth month of pregnancy\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa300\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf2 Others can now feel your baby move, which may mean more hands on your belly than before. The fatigue is probably growing and you may also suffer from insomnia, which is an unfortunate combination. Remember to nap when you can, even if it\'92s not during traditional sleep hours. You may notice that your breasts have a slight discharge or film on them; this is normal and is just your body\'92s way of prepping for the baby. Your uterus may be so big that you have trouble breathing.
\f0\b \cf4 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Ninth month of pregnancy\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa300\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf2 Your body is now in get-ready mode. Your {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.acog.org/Patients/FAQs/How-to-Tell-When-Labor-Begins"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 cervix will start changing}} at some point in these last weeks, depending on whether you\'92ve had a baby before, by softening and moving forward. The uterus will practice contracting until the big day. You may notice an increase in vaginal discharge. This is normal as long as it isn\'92t green and doesn\'92t have an offensive odor. Your comfort really depends on the baby\'92s position. For example, a low, head-down baby means better breathing, but more trips to the bathroom.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 Overdue pregnancy\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa300\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf2 Many women will see their due date come and go without giving birth. In the week or two past your due date, the changes in your body all point to labor ahead. For some, these are obvious; for others, they may be subtle or non-existent. \'93Bloody show\'94 is when the mucus plug dislodges from the cervix, which indicates that the cervix is changing, but not necessarily that it\'92s time for labor. Comfort measures can be very helpful, as can distraction techniques.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 The beginning of labor\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa300\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf2 The changes that occur in the body toward the end of pregnancy slowly slip into the start of labor. Labor is defined by contractions that are regular and dilate the cervix, ending in the birth of the baby. You may notice a backache or flu-like symptoms. Most women will go into labor on their own between weeks 38 and 42. There are times when there is a medical condition with mom or baby that requires medical help to get labor to start.\
\'97\
**Second Month (Weeks 5-8)**:\
- **Development**: The embryo starts forming major organs like the heart, liver, and lungs.\
- **Symptoms**: A pregnancy test will most likely confirm the pregnancy as periods might be missed. Fatigue is prominent due to hormonal changes. Increased urination, heartburn, and morning sickness (nausea and vomiting) might be present.\
\
**Third Month (Weeks 9-12)**:\
- **Development**: Organs continue developing and the fetus's limbs, fingers, toes, and facial features become more defined.\
- **Symptoms**: The uterus grows but will only reach the top of the pelvis by the end of this month. Weight gain is minimal or none, with some women losing weight due to sickness or food aversions. A decrease in libido, glowing skin, dark facial blotches, or acne might occur.\
\
**Fourth Month (Weeks 13-16)**:\
- **Development**: Fetus can now suck its thumb and make facial expressions.\
- **Symptoms**: Women start feeling more energized and symptoms like morning sickness might diminish. Relief might be due to decreased risk of early miscarriage and hormonal stabilization. There is often a return of pre-pregnancy sex drive levels.\
\
**Fifth Month (Weeks 17-20)**:\
- **Development**: Vital organs like the heart and kidneys continue to develop.\
- **Symptoms**: Expanded blood volume results in a faster heart rate. Increased urination persists. Baby's movements, or "quickening", are likely felt for the first time. The uterus reaches the belly button, drawing attention to the pregnancy.\
\
**Sixth Month (Weeks 21-24)**:\
- **Development**: The baby's bones are fully formed but remain soft.\
- **Symptoms**: A visible baby bump and frequent heartburn due to reduced stomach space. Distinct baby movements can be felt. Maternity clothes become a staple, and some women may experience mild backaches.\
\
**Seventh Month (Weeks 25-28)**:\
- **Development**: The baby's brain and lungs continue to mature.\
- **Symptoms**: The realization that the baby's arrival is approaching might lead to increased labor planning. Reduced sexual interest, fatigue, and practice contractions known as Braxton-Hicks contractions may occur.\
\
**Eighth Month (Weeks 29-32)**:\
- **Development**: The baby continues gaining weight and accumulating fat.\
- **Symptoms**: Others might feel the baby's movements when touching the mother's belly. Increased fatigue paired with insomnia is common. A possible discharge or film on the breasts is the body's preparation for breastfeeding. The growing uterus can cause breathing difficulties.\
\
**Ninth Month (Weeks 33-36 and up to birth)**:\
- **Development**: The baby's brain and lungs are nearly fully developed.\
- **Symptoms**: Cervical changes in preparation for birth. The uterus practices contractions. Increased vaginal discharge is common, with its characteristics being indicators of potential issues. Comfort varies depending on the baby's position.\
\
**Overdue Pregnancy**:\
- **Symptoms**: Many women might exceed their due date without giving birth. Signs of impending labor could range from obvious to subtle. "Bloody show", the dislodging of the cervical mucus plug, hints at cervical changes but not necessarily imminent labor. Comfort measures and distractions can be helpful.\
\
**Beginning of Labor**:\
- **Symptoms**: The end-of-pregnancy changes morph into labor. Labor consists of regular contractions that dilate the cervix, culminating in the baby's birth. Initial signs might be a backache or flu-like symptoms. Most women naturally enter labor between weeks 38 and 42, but medical intervention might be necessary due to specific conditions.\
\
Always consult with a healthcare provider for a comprehensive understanding and guidance throughout pregnancy.\
\'97\
Pregnancy is a unique experience for every woman, but there are common developmental milestones and symptoms associated with each month. Here's a general overview of the month-by-month experiences of pregnancy:\
\
1. **Month 1 (Weeks 1-4)**:\
   - **Development**: The fertilized egg implants itself into the uterus.\
   - **Symptoms**: Many women don't realize they're pregnant yet. Some might experience missed periods, breast tenderness, fatigue, or frequent urination.\
\
2. **Month 2 (Weeks 5-8)**:\
   - **Development**: The embryo starts forming major organs like the heart, liver, and lungs. The heart begins to beat.\
   - **Symptoms**: Morning sickness (nausea and vomiting), changes in food aversions or sensitivities, fatigue, breast tenderness, and mood swings.\
\
3. **Month 3 (Weeks 9-12)**:\
   - **Development**: Limbs, fingers, toes, and facial features become more defined. By week 12, the fetus is fully formed.\
   - **Symptoms**: Morning sickness might continue, breasts may grow larger, and some women experience increased energy levels.\
\
4. **Month 4 (Weeks 13-16)**:\
   - **Development**: The fetus can suck its thumb, make facial expressions, and the kidneys start producing urine.\
   - **Symptoms**: Many women feel better from the intense symptoms of the first trimester. There may be visible weight gain, and the "baby bump" may start to show.\
\
5. **Month 5 (Weeks 17-20)**:\
   - **Development**: Baby's movements can often be felt by the mother, known as "quickening." The baby's skin is covered in vernix and lanugo.\
   - **Symptoms**: Increased appetite, continued weight gain, and possible leg cramps or backaches. Some women have the "anatomy scan" ultrasound around 20 weeks.\
\
6. **Month 6 (Weeks 21-24)**:\
   - **Development**: The baby's lungs are developing, but they're not ready for outside life yet.\
   - **Symptoms**: The belly continues to grow, and stretch marks might appear. Some women experience swelling in the feet or ankles.\
\
7. **Month 7 (Weeks 25-28)**:\
   - **Development**: The baby's eyes can open and close, and it can respond to sounds.\
   - **Symptoms**: Shortness of breath, heartburn, and more frequent urination. The baby's movements become stronger and more regular.\
\
8. **Month 8 (Weeks 29-32)**:\
   - **Development**: The baby gains weight and layers of fat, and its bones are fully formed but soft.\
   - **Symptoms**: Braxton Hicks contractions (false labor pains) might start. Some women experience pelvic pain or feel more tired.\
\
9. **Month 9 (Weeks 33-36 and up to birth)**:\
   - **Development**: The baby's lungs and brain continue to mature. Most babies will turn into a head-down position for birth.\
   - **Symptoms**: More discomfort, including backaches, pelvic pain, and difficulty sleeping. Increased frequency of doctor visits. By the end of this period, labor could start at any time.\
\
Keep in mind that every pregnancy is unique. Some women may experience symptoms not listed here, or they might not experience some of the common symptoms. Always consult with a healthcare provider about any concerns during pregnancy.}